    chapters: [{ number: "01", label: "THE PROBLEM", title: "A bottle can be beautiful and still be unknown.", body: "Sensory Discovery begins with the sensory distance of a new fragrance. A perfect packshot does not give a person a reason to imagine the memory, place, or feeling that a scent might carry." }, { number: "02", label: "THE CLUE", title: "Let the launch arrive in fragments.", body: "The client launch system uses a deliberate sequence of material, colour, object, note, invitation, and first-person discovery. Each piece gives a small amount of meaning and leaves space for a person’s own association." }, { number: "03", label: "THE MEMORY", title: "Recognition should feel earned, not announced.", body: "The intended result is a cultural sense of having discovered something rather than being told that it matters. This is a creative premise, not a claim about sell-through, awareness, or campaign performance." }],
